WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:09.300
SIERRA: Just for fun, would you be up for
like lightning round ranking the following

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.339
popular spy portrayals on a scale of like
1 to 10 for accuracy?

00:00:13.339 --> 00:00:16.160
LUCAS: If I've seen them, I'll do my best.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.220
SIERRA: Okay, so obviously, we have to start
with James Bond.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.970
LUCAS: Am I allowed to give negative scores?

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:22.970
SIERRA: Absolutely you are.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:29.250
LUCAS: I mean, James Bond bears as much relation
to espionage as The Lion King does to natural

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:35.700
history. But it's great for the MI6 brand.
They never complain.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.750
SIERRA: Good recruitment. Okay. What about
The Americans?

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:43.530
LUCAS: I think quite plausible. There are
Russian illegals and from what we know about

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.129
them, that wasn't, it's not as exciting as
the Americans but I would give that a six.

00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:52.670
SIERRA: Ooh, okay. That's great. What about
the Mission Impossible franchise?

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:58.090
LUCAS: Bambi. I'm surprised you didn t ask
me about the Bourne Identity.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.100
SIERRA: Go ahead, what is it?

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:05.610
LUCAS: I've done Bambi, and I've done The
Lion King. I think this is Pinocchio.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:06.610
SIERRA: This is why I didn't ask.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:07.610
Bridge of Spies: In the name of God, why are
we hanging him .he s a spy.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:08.670
The Spy Who Came In From the Cold: What the
hell do you think spies are? Moral philosophers

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.700
measuring everything they do against the word
of God or Karl Marx? They re not.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:24.280
The Americans: You re going to have to make
a decision to commit to this life or get out.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:33.520
It s not easy and it doesn t always end well.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:39.869
SIERRA: Spy content is huge in popular culture.
Ever since the Cold War, the appetite for

00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:46.450
spy books, spy movies, and spy shows has been
insatiable. But behind the Hollywood treatment

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:51.549
is a real profession. It consumes billions
of dollars a year, and it fuels the decisions

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:56.580
of world leaders and militaries on a daily
basis. Without it, countries would be left

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.850
vulnerable to catastrophe.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:04.549
And yet it remains deeply misunderstood. Most
of the Hollywood images bear almost no resemblance

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:10.479
to the daily work of the intelligence community,
known to insiders as the IC . The reality

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:16.280
is fascinating, in its own way, and the reality
is also changing in the era of the internet,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:22.370
spyware, and artificial intelligence. I m
Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It Matters.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:29.150
Today, how spying really works.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:39.450
SIERRA: I think we should start by getting
our terminology in order. What is espionage?

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:45.760
LUCAS: Espionage is finding stuff out, usually
on behalf of governments, and usually involving

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.940
some kind of rule-breaking. If it's not involving
rule-breaking, then it's basically journalism.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.100
SIERRA: This is Edward Lucas, he s a journalist
and a Senior Fellow at the Center for European

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.110
Policy Analysis. He has written several books
about espionage.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:04.150
SIERRA: So then, by contrast, what is intelligence?

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:11.010
LUCAS: Intelligence is the product of espionage,
strictly speaking, so you can have raw intelligence,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.260
which is just the stuff that your spies or
your code crackers have found out, and then

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.870
that's processed and analyzed, verified, and
then becomes intelligence product, which is

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:24.599
what is sent to decision-makers, for example,
in the Presidential Daily Brief. And then

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:30.170
that hopefully, allows them to make better-informed
decisions about the threats and opportunities

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.260
facing their country.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:39.099
HARDING: Intelligence is this somewhat amorphous
broad concept that means finding the information

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:44.040
that your government needs wherever it happens
to reside, pulling it together, and presenting

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.160
it in a usable fashion.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.310
SIERRA: This is Emily Harding. She used to
be an analyst at the CIA, where she eventually

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:57.260
led the Iraq Group. She was also deputy director
of the Senate Select Committee on Intelligence

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:02.680
and worked on its investigation of Russian
interference in the 2016 election. Nowadays

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:07.370
she is a Senior Fellow and Deputy Director
at the Center for Strategic and International

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:08.420
Studies.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.690
HARDING: You want to be able to say hey, there's
this big development out there that's coming

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:17.570
that you need to be ready for. You wanna try
to reduce uncertainty for the policymakers

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:23.260
so that they can try to make the best policies
possible. Espionage is one way to go about

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:29.199
gathering that intelligence. So espionage
is going about stealing the secrets generally

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:35.421
speaking from people. So recruiting an asset
in the field who can do something like, tell

00:04:35.421 --> 00:04:40.510
you about the intentions of a foreign government.
Those are often the hardest things to learn

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:45.000
and collect. What is a foreign government
planning to do? What are they intending to

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.979
do with their policy? That's not the kind
of thing that you can usually get from open-source

00:04:49.979 --> 00:04:54.259
or figure out in other ways. You really need
somebody who's well-placed on the inside to

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:59.690
just tell you these things. And so we have
very well-trained professionals who go all

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:04.590
around the world and try to recruit assets
to conduct espionage.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:11.660
SIERRA: Spies like jargon. And along the way,
we are going to have to break a bit of that

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:16.870
down for you. So, in the movies, James Bond
will typically break into the enemy headquarters

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:22.540
to steal a key piece of information or technology.
In real life, that s actually not a very effective

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:28.949
strategy. Most real-world spying is done by
assets. That is someone who is already inside

00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:34.820
the organization, city, or country you are
spying on. Someone who has access, someone

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:40.000
who blends in. And how do you convince someone
to spy on their own country? Well, that is

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.160
the work of an operations officer. These are
the people working for the CIA, or some other

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:50.860
agency, who have the tough job of finding
people to provide or steal secrets. They recruit

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.530
them, monitor them, protect them, and when
the time is right, assist them in obtaining

00:05:55.530 --> 00:06:01.210
the information. That information is then
funneled back to the CIA, or another agency,

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:06.199
where it is processed by analysts. These are
the people who interpret and piece together

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:11.530
bits of intelligence, kind of like a puzzle.
Each role is important, and each serves a

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:15.000
very specific purpose.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:22.510
LUCAS: On the whole, espionage is much less
interesting than anything in fiction, because

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:27.070
so much of it takes so long. And so sitting
around waiting for things to happen is a very

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:32.170
important part of the espionage world and
acting very slowly, because mistakes are very

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:36.480
expensive and can be lethal. That doesn't
come across very well when you're looking

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.039
at trying to fill a 90 minute Hollywood blockbuster.

00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:40.039
SIERRA: No definitely not.

00:06:40.039 --> 00:06:44.780
LUCAS: But I suppose the key thing is that
the people who do the spying usually are recruited

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:50.500
by intelligence officers and so the intelligence
officers job is to find a potential source,

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:55.090
to recruit them, to run them, if necessary
to get them out of trouble if they're close

00:06:55.090 --> 00:07:00.160
to getting caught. And those are all serious
skills, you have to be really good at doing

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.940
that. Actually, stealing the secret may be
a matter of emptying waste paper baskets at

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:09.340
the right time, or sticking a USB drive into
a computer, or doing something other that

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:15.699
can be rather less skilled and less glamorous.
So when we say spying, that encompasses both

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:21.759
the actual stealing of the secret, and then
the huge array of highly trained intelligence

00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:25.979
officers who cause all that to happen, and
those are two slightly different things.

00:07:25.979 --> 00:07:31.630
SIERRA: So let's zoom out to 30,000 feet just
for a moment. What is the ultimate goal of

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:34.900
espionage? Why do governments need it?

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:40.759
LUCAS: The ultimate goal is better decision-making.
That's the most important thing and then quite

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:44.410
an important secondary goal is to find out
if other countries are spying on you. Because

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:48.849
if they know your secrets, and you don't know
theirs, they will negotiate better.

00:07:48.849 --> 00:07:54.669
SIERRA: Intelligence successes typically go
unsung - the Allied forces cracking the Enigma

00:07:54.669 --> 00:08:01.669
code during World War II, U.S. intelligence
identifying missiles on Cuba in 1962, and

00:08:01.669 --> 00:08:08.510
the CIA predicting the outcome to the 1967
Arab-Israeli War, to name a few. We hear a

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:13.949
lot more about the failures. The 9/11 Commission
Report identified a series of intelligence

00:08:13.949 --> 00:08:18.830
shortcomings in the run-up to the attack,
and these failings became a permanent part

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:24.140
of how the tragedy is publically understood.
When intelligence does its job, almost nobody

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:27.820
knows. When it fails, everyone does.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:32.539
HARDING: Pearl Harbor is the first case study
that they teach you when you come on board

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:36.300
at The Agency as an analyst, where they talk
about why you need intelligence, why you need

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:41.310
intelligence analysts. With Pearl Harbor,
there were a lot of indications and warnings

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:46.560
out there about what the Japanese might be
planning to do, that there was perhaps a large

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:51.330
attack underway. But because that information
was scattered to a bunch of different places

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:56.089
around what was then the Defense Department,
nobody really pulled them all together to

00:08:56.089 --> 00:09:00.839
come up with a complete picture and be able
to provide that indication and warning. So

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:06.779
they created the OSS, and the OSS then evolved
to become the CIA. And the goal really was

00:09:06.779 --> 00:09:11.980
to take all these little pieces of information,
pull them together for a coherent picture,

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:17.589
and then provide something usable to the policymaker.
A good friend of mine says that intelligence

00:09:17.589 --> 00:09:22.380
work is less about connecting the dots and
more about pointillism, like those famous

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:26.060
pieces of artwork. Where you take all these
little tiny pieces of information you have,

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:31.200
and you try to pull them together into a coherent
picture. So that's why we need intelligence.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:37.000
Some of those dots are open source. Some of
those dots are in the media or discoverable

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.830
out there on the internet, but a great many
of them also are highly secret. They come

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:47.180
from those human spies that we were talking
about. They come from signals intelligence

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:52.500
when you pull up conversations or you pull
metadata from electronic communications. And

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.730
those kinds of capabilities need to be kept
secret for various reasons. So that's why

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:57.820
we need an intelligence service.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:04.570
SIERRA: All right. So this sounds like a ton
of people, a ton of steps a lot of time. So

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:09.560
I guess the question is how much does this
all cost?

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:16.780
HARDING: Billions of dollars, but it's a drop
in the bucket compared to the defense budget.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:23.120
SIERRA: When Emily says billions, she means
billions. In 2020, the intelligence community

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:29.980
appropriated $85.8 billion dollars. That s
a lot, almost four times as much as the government

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:36.730
spent on the space program. But, as Emily
points out, it s only a fraction of the $778

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:40.279
billion that the country spent on overall
defense last year.

00:10:40.279 --> 00:10:44.980
HARDING: I think that people tend to focus
on the size of the defense budget and think

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:49.600
about, you know, the big ships and things.
And those are all game-changers in the United

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.500
States' security picture, but with the intelligence
community, you get a lot of bang for your

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:01.300
buck. Some collection programs are exceedingly
expensive. You know, we have exquisite satellites

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:07.760
that do really cool things and they cost lots
and lots of money. We also have very low-cost

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:12.529
partnerships with commercial companies that
are collecting satellite imagery as well.

00:11:12.529 --> 00:11:17.220
And we put those two pictures together to
come up with a good coherent picture for our

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:22.420
policymakers. So it's expensive. But you definitely
get information that you won t get anywhere

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:23.420
else.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:28.839
SIERRA: And there are a lot of different organizations
collecting that information. Right now, there

00:11:28.839 --> 00:11:35.260
are 18 intelligence agencies within the US,
including Space Force Intelligence, which

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:39.650
was added this year. You may not have even
heard of several of them before, like The

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:45.839
National Geospatial Agency, which used satellite
and drone technology to map Osama Bin Laden's

00:11:45.839 --> 00:11:49.760
compound, allowing Navy SEALs to simulate
and prepare for their mission.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:54.089
HARDING: The way that it works is that there
are intelligence agencies that are connected

00:11:54.089 --> 00:11:59.430
to each one of the military services, and
then also small intelligence agencies that

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:03.639
serve kind of a niche function connected to
a bunch of different departments and agencies.

00:12:03.639 --> 00:12:08.420
So like the Department of Energy has its own
tiny intelligence service. The Department

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:12.670
of State has its own intelligence service.
And each one of those smaller agencies is

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:18.649
very tightly focused on the needs of the top
customers in their building. On the other

00:12:18.649 --> 00:12:22.970
hand, there are also big overarching what
we call all source intelligence agencies like

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:28.450
CIA and DIA, that are looking at all the different
sources of information and trying to pull

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:31.079
them together for a broader customer set.

00:12:31.079 --> 00:12:35.460
SIERRA: Along with the Central Intelligence
Agency and the Defence Intelligence Agency,

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:41.440
there s another major player: the NSA, or
National Security Agency. The joke used to

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:47.280
be that those letters stood for no such agency
, because of its extreme secrecy. The NSA

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:52.730
s original purpose was to protect secret messages
from other government agencies and to break

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:59.430
enemy codes. These days it has gotten very,
very good at hacking. With each of these agencies,

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:04.750
there is a constant churn, millions of data
points, thousands of analysts interpreting

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:11.190
what s found. All of it feeding a constant
series of decisions large and small.

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:16.459
?SIERRA: So who uses all of this intelligence?
Is it the President?

00:13:16.459 --> 00:13:21.040
HARDING: So the president isn't the biggest
consumer by quantity. The president certainly

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:25.850
gets anything that he wants, gets the most
classified information, the least classified

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:30.300
information. Any question the president has
gets answered. As far as quantity goes, though,

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:36.410
it has to be the US Military. They get both
the tactical intelligence from the battlefield

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:41.940
like, "What's over that next hill? What does
the enemy planning on doing tomorrow? What

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:46.180
IEDs are waiting for us out on the battlefield,"
they get that super tactical intelligence

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:50.690
picture, but then they also get the strategic
intelligence picture like for example, with

00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:55.950
the current case of Afghanistan, "Are the
Taliban planning to negotiate in good faith?

00:13:55.950 --> 00:14:02.910
What role is Pakistan playing in those negotiations?
What potential interests does Iran have in

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:08.370
the US withdrawal from Afghanistan?" So the
military is taking in intelligence at every

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:15.600
single level and at various levels of detail
as well, to serve a vast need for information.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:20.339
SIERRA: As a side note, it s worth mentioning
that since these interviews were recorded,

00:14:20.339 --> 00:14:24.899
there has been an intense debate about the
role of intelligence in the U.S. withdrawal

00:14:24.899 --> 00:14:29.089
from Afghanistan, as well as the future of
our intelligence capabilities in the region.

00:14:29.089 --> 00:14:34.579
It s a deep and complex issue, and it could
be the topic of its own episode. We won t

00:14:34.579 --> 00:14:40.110
be getting into it today, but you can find
a lot of related coverage on CFR.org if you

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:41.510
re interested.

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:49.490
SIERRA: Ok so to just go back to the President,
how does he or she receive and use intelligence?

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:55.410
HARDING: So the president, being the president,
gets to know anything he or she wants. Every

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:59.700
day the intelligence community puts together
what we call the book for the president. And

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:07.399
the book will include anywhere from five to
10 pieces that talk about strategic developments,

00:15:07.399 --> 00:15:12.769
that talk about tactical developments, that
reveal what's going on around the world. The

00:15:12.769 --> 00:15:16.550
president might spend an hour in the morning
working on the PDB and getting their intelligence

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:22.860
briefing. However, somebody like the DNI who's
now Avril Haines, their only job is working

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:25.079
on the business of intelligence.

00:15:25.079 --> 00:15:32.459
SIERRA: The DNI, or Director of National Intelligence,
was a role created in 2004 to oversee and

00:15:32.459 --> 00:15:38.630
centralize a complex network of U.S. intelligence
agencies. It was developed in response to

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:43.730
9/11. They are responsible for promoting the
sharing of information by easing institutional

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:45.800
and operational barriers.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:51.480
HARDING: But it's still the president's intelligence
and he can have anything he wants. Really

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:58.339
each president crafts this process exactly
how he likes it. So President Obama, he really

00:15:58.339 --> 00:16:01.800
enjoyed reading, so he would take his book
and actually do a lot of reading of the book

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:06.279
and do a lot of notes on it. President Trump
apparently was more into graphics and liked

00:16:06.279 --> 00:16:10.880
graphic depictions of what was going on and
wanted to talk things through. President Bush,

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:17.620
the second President Bush, he was a voracious
consumer of intelligence. And he really liked

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:21.779
not only a really robust book to read, but
he liked to have conversations. So he would

00:16:21.779 --> 00:16:26.560
actually invite analysts into the Oval to
have conversations about the pieces that they'd

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:31.449
written to do a deep dive on a particular
subject. So each president really shapes the

00:16:31.449 --> 00:16:37.290
PDB the way that they most want to use it
and the way that's most useful for them.

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:42.449
SIERRA: That's a lot of power in one person's
hands to decide.

00:16:42.449 --> 00:16:43.449
HARDING: It is.

00:16:43.449 --> 00:16:44.870
SIERRA: What they hear and pay attention to.

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:50.959
HARDING: It is. And there's always this very
careful dance between telling the President

00:16:50.959 --> 00:16:54.529
things that he's gonna be interested in or
they're gonna be useful, and then telling

00:16:54.529 --> 00:16:59.120
him things that he really doesn't want to
hear. The story about telling truth to power,

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:01.570
that old adage, it's 100% true.

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:06.380
SIERRA: So do separate agencies ever produce
conflicting information?

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:13.570
HARDING: Yes. So this was one of the big takeaways
after 9/11 and also after the Iraq War. What

00:17:13.570 --> 00:17:20.169
you want in the intelligence community is
not duplication, not redundancy, but a really

00:17:20.169 --> 00:17:26.180
robust exchange of views. And it makes sense
for several agencies to be looking at the

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:32.150
same sets of data and then perhaps taking
different interpretations or competing interpretations.

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:38.160
One of the things that is required as an analyst
at CIA, is that before you write any piece,

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:43.490
you have to send your draft around to the
entire inter-agency, all the intelligence

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:49.340
agencies and say, Any comments? And if somebody
comes back and says, Well, I think you've

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:53.880
misinterpreted this point. Or I think that
you should adjust this language to be more

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:57.669
precise. Then you try your best to incorporate
all of those comments. But every once in a

00:17:57.669 --> 00:18:02.760
while somebody will come back and say, I think
you've got this totally wrong, you're totally

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:07.740
missing the point here. Your analysis is bad.
And I think it's actually this instead. Then

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:12.880
we publish something called a dissent. And
that's really important for the intellectual

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:18.700
honesty of intelligence analysis. And also
just to let the policymaker know that there's

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:20.970
not one way to look at this.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:27.440
SIERRA: A high-priority briefing could influence
a president s decision to take military action.

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:32.020
But intelligence also gets used in things
as seemingly innocuous as trade negotiations.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:37.490
At the military level, a briefing might be
about something as granular as whether a bridge

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:42.531
can bear the weight of a tank crossing it,
and as far-reaching as nuclear security. The

00:18:42.531 --> 00:18:47.830
flow has been there for a long time. But the
methods, challenges, and opportunities for

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:52.860
the intelligence community have been changing.
And to understand how spying really happens

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:57.679
these days, we have to start with the old
days of World War II and the Cold War that

00:18:57.679 --> 00:19:02.820
followed. You know, the era of Russian and
American spies that inspired so many classic

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:09.370
novels and films. Tinker, Tailor, Soldier,
Spy: I suppose that s where it began, turning

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:16.080
my eyes to the East, oh so how trivial we
ve become as a nation, say mid-40s...

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:20.360
SIERRA: Can you give me a quick summary of,
sort of, the good old days of espionage?

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:26.720
LUCAS: In the old days, you could pretend
to be someone just by claiming to be them.

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:30.820
You could say I am so-and-so and if you spoke
the right language and had the right clothes

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:35.590
and seemed quite confident. It was quite hard
for any before the days of say, fingerprints,

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:38.960
and photographs, it was really quite hard
to check if someone wasn't who they said they

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:43.669
were. So perhaps the greatest British spy
ever, Paul Dukes happened to speak really,

00:19:43.669 --> 00:19:48.970
really good Russian. And he turned up in Russia
after the revolution, and actually played

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:55.169
the piano in the canteen of the secret police
headquarters, which is a perfect place for

00:19:55.169 --> 00:20:00.919
him to run his agents. He borrowed money from
British merchants who are still hanging on

00:20:00.919 --> 00:20:06.460
there hoping that communism was temporary.
And he was perhaps the most successful British

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:11.660
spy ever. And everything he did would be completely
impossible now. Because he was able to bluff

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:15.309
his way into things where nowadays they'd
be saying, well, hang on where your documents?

00:20:15.309 --> 00:20:19.200
How do you get into the country? Where's your
passport? All these all these questions. And

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.360
so the ability to pretend to be someone who
you're not, which is absolutely central to

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:28.690
espionage is getting harder all the time.

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:34.030
SIERRA: So you mentioned that things have
changed since the old days. And I want to

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:38.630
understand, why? So let's start with technology.

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:45.620
LUCAS: Technology means that we leave a kind
of digital snail's trail, everywhere we go,

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:50.289
you carry a mobile phone with you, probably
2 billion people in the world, have mobile

00:20:50.289 --> 00:20:55.450
phones or access to them, carry them with
them all of the time, we live our lives on

00:20:55.450 --> 00:21:00.520
social media, we have Instagram accounts and
LinkedIn, 750 million LinkedIn, 2 billion

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:05.710
on Facebook. And we have credit ratings. Very
particularly in the advanced industrial societies,

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:11.350
we pay with electronic money, credit cards,
for example, or online payments. And all this

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:16.190
leaves a kind of digital trail completely
innocent, in most cases, it's not particularly

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:19.690
interesting to know that I got up in the morning,
I went out, I bought a coffee with a card,

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:23.500
and I went swimming and didn't take my phone
with me. But I was no doubt caught on the

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:27.770
CCTV at the swimming place, and so on. But
you can put all these things together, and

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:31.529
they start building a very clear picture.
So you say, who is this person who doesn't

00:21:31.529 --> 00:21:35.590
have a mobile phone? That's really surprising.
If someone has a mobile phone, then that's

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:39.630
basically a tracking beacon, you can say,
when's it been switched on last? Where do

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:44.190
they go? Even if you can't get access to the
actual calls and texts and things that they've

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:49.890
used the mobile phone for just as a geolocation
device, it's a really powerful tool. And the

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:53.900
absence of it is a really powerful tool. So
you put all these things together, and it

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:58.590
becomes really hard to be anonymous, particularly
if you then add the biometrics, the facial

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:02.700
recognition, the way in which you can make
a computer image of the way someone walks,

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:07.290
so-called gate analysis. And you just see
only one person, the world walks like that.

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:11.020
And he's walking down the street in Beijing
right now, and we want to know why, bang,

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:15.409
you know, that's a really powerful tool for
the counterintelligence people.

00:22:15.409 --> 00:22:20.580
SIERRA: That's wild. I love that the technology
points out that if someone has a lack of presence

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:24.730
on those things, it's just as suspicious.
I feel the same way, when I look up someone

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:29.990
my friends are dating and I'm like, well,
they're not on these things, seem suspicious.

00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:32.320
So I guess that translates all the way up
to this as well.

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:37.120
LUCAS: Yes, I mean, it used to be that it
was quite easy to create a sort of plausible

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:42.480
seeming identity, that would stand up to really
quite a lot of scrutiny. And now it's really

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:46.950
difficult if you turn up in Russia, or in
China, pretending to be someone you're not

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:52.380
the consequences of getting it wrong are catastrophic.
And the margin for error is very, very small.

00:22:52.380 --> 00:23:00.460
SIERRA: Is it also that technology has provided
a cheaper, safer way of getting information?

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:06.230
LUCAS: Yes, one of the sort of sayings in
the Espionage world is that when one door

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:12.700
closes, another door opens. And we're able
to find out a lot more about our adversaries,

00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:16.110
thanks to technology. So the sort of things
that Bellingcat has done, and they're not

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:19.960
an intelligence agency, that were kind of
private intelligence agency. But they've been

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:26.190
able to catch Russian spies by basically googling
and buying commercially available information.

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:31.240
And it's really impressive what you can do.
So this is clearly something that helps us

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:36.240
on the counterintelligence side. But in terms
of aggressive intelligence collection, if

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:40.730
you're trying to get alongside say, someone
in China who's got access to secrets when

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:44.100
you want to get hold of them in a way that
the Chinese authorities are never going to

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:48.390
find out. That is becoming really difficult.
It's not impossible, but it's really difficult.

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:50.520
SIERRA: And I would assume more dangerous.

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:54.640
LUCAS: Dangerous for them certainly, and in
fact that there was a very serious breach

00:23:54.640 --> 00:24:01.000
in the CIA's communication software a few
years ago, which led to every single, it is

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:05.900
said, CIA assets in China being caught and
executed. Because the way that they had for

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:11.820
their agents to communicate, involved a brilliantly
thought out piece of kind of anonymous software.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:16.340
But the trouble is, once the Chinese worked
out what it was, they were then able to spot

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:22.620
everyone who was using it. So the potential
for catastrophe is much, much higher And increasingly,

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:26.030
people are going for different ways of doing
intelligence rather than the old-fashioned

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:27.150
cover identity.

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:31.960
SIERRA: Are there specific ways that artificial
intelligence in particular is changing the

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:32.960
game?

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:36.600
LUCAS: Absolutely. Artificial Intelligence
is the secret sauce here because we've known

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:41.370
for ages that it's possible to collect huge
amounts of data. You hack into Facebook, and

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:45.810
you get the 2 billion Facebook accounts and
you can, presumably, the Chinese can do that.

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:50.620
What makes it really impressive is when you
look for the anomalies. And so you see, it's

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:54.789
put together that with the mobile phone database,
and lots of sort of Google searches. And I

00:24:54.789 --> 00:25:00.260
want to find everyone who's been to China,
whose face appears under one account on Facebook,

00:25:00.260 --> 00:25:04.150
and who is using a burner phone when they
come to China or something like that. And

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:09.140
that's the sort of thing that AI is really
good at spotting the oddities. And the oddities

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:13.539
may just be somebody lives a very odd life
and happens to use a different burner phone

00:25:13.539 --> 00:25:17.900
every time they go on a foreign trip or happens
to like having Facebook identities that are

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:24.169
not their own. But on the whole, these oddities
are tremendously powerful clues for counter-intelligence

00:25:24.169 --> 00:25:28.940
people. And the development of AI means we
get better and better at finding them in big

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:30.220
sets of data.

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:35.679
HARDING: So, Compute really is the future
of the intelligence enterprise. There will

00:25:35.679 --> 00:25:41.649
never be a substitution for, you know, somebody
recruiting the exact right asset at the exact

00:25:41.649 --> 00:25:46.990
right time, who's gonna tell you what the
adversary government is thinking. But, there

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:54.870
is so much information out there in the world
just sitting and waiting to be used and there

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:59.029
are some companies and corporations out there
who are already starting to compete with the

00:25:59.029 --> 00:26:04.540
intelligence community on taking all the information
and compiling it in a usable form and that

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:09.940
is going to be I think, a competition challenge
for the IC. They're gonna have to figure out

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:13.920
what do we buy off the shelf from some of
these companies and then what do we try and,

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:19.680
and recreate in house ourselves and where
does the secret line really come in? So, in

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:24.360
my job right now I'm working on a whole set
of projects on how to make better use of what

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:30.840
you call open-source intelligence. And, this
is traditionally you know, like The New York

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:31.840
Times.

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:32.840
SIERRA:
Right.

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:34.470
HARDING:
Or you know, some kind of foreign broadcasting

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:39.710
services information. But, now it's much more
than that. It's the digital dust that's scattered

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:46.820
all over the internet and how can you lien
usable information from that digital dust.

00:26:46.820 --> 00:26:51.830
I think that as we move forward, we're gonna
need those data scientists, we're gonna need

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:57.539
those coders, we're gonna need the really
super talented hackers who are just desperately

00:26:57.539 --> 00:27:03.111
clever at getting around security constraints
and stealing the secrets that way. And there

00:27:03.111 --> 00:27:09.919
just are not enough data scientists out there
to both feed the giant sucking sound in Silicon

00:27:09.919 --> 00:27:14.870
Valley, and then also feed the US government
and specifically the intelligence community.

00:27:14.870 --> 00:27:19.899
And, you know, speaking as a former government
employee, I think what we were always really

00:27:19.899 --> 00:27:24.669
hoping for when I was hiring people, was that
the mission would be compelling and would

00:27:24.669 --> 00:27:26.870
pull in the best talent.

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:30.630
SIERRA:
What do you see as the biggest intelligence

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:33.710
priorities for the US right now?

00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:37.090
HARDING:
Like I said, the Russians are the Russians

00:27:37.090 --> 00:27:42.149
and you gotta give a lot of respect to those
guys for the work that they've done, you know

00:27:42.149 --> 00:27:47.919
my work on the 2016 election at the same time
as I really hate everything that they did,

00:27:47.919 --> 00:27:52.140
you also gotta give them some grudging respect
for how well they did some of it.

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:56.669
I would say though that the number one intelligence
challenge for us right now is China. And,

00:27:56.669 --> 00:28:03.710
that's because they are talented at what they
do. Their intelligence services are very talented.

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:11.730
But, they also have figured out a way to forcibly
co-opt huge percentages of their population

00:28:11.730 --> 00:28:17.659
to help the state. So, if you're a Chinese
business, if you are a business based in China,

00:28:17.659 --> 00:28:21.570
maybe you're not officially a state-owned
enterprise but, you know where your bread

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:26.090
is buttered and if the Chinese government
comes to you and says "I need A, B, and C."

00:28:26.090 --> 00:28:33.059
Then you're gonna give it to them. So, I think
that, that China is both smart and well resourced

00:28:33.059 --> 00:28:39.320
and we as Americans are just not used to the
concept that somebody who says they're here

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:43.460
to do business is also working on behalf of
the government and it's taken us a minute

00:28:43.460 --> 00:28:46.250
to get smart on that and to really recognize
the threat.

00:28:46.250 --> 00:28:53.740
SIERRA: It can be hard to know what to make
of all of this. As the 18 intelligence services

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:58.880
of the United States compete with parallel
organizations in places like Russia and China,

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:04.260
most of what happens remains secret. We know
from the past that there have been mistakes,

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:09.250
and even injustices, carried out in the name
of intelligence. On the other hand, we don

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:13.020
t have a way of knowing how many times tragedies
have been averted.

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:16.870
SIERRA:
So it seems like secrecy is the thing here.

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:22.590
So as everyday people, how can we assess the
value of espionage if we never get to know

00:29:22.590 --> 00:29:26.080
the details and aren't really aware of the
results? Should we just trust that the world

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:28.870
has been saved without us knowing about it?

00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:32.830
LUCAS:
Well, I think what you can certainly do is

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:37.950
question your elected representatives about
the amount of money that's going in. I think

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:43.690
that one can question the results in terms
of, is my country safer, and when there's

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:47.890
clearly something gone very badly wrong, as
it did with 9/11. You can say Well, hey, you

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:50.809
know, you're paid all this money to catch
these people. Why didn't you? And so after

00:29:50.809 --> 00:29:55.950
the event, if something's gone wrong, it's
harder to ask when things have gone, right,

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:59.659
because obviously, the sources and methods
involved are quite precious and one doesn't

00:29:59.659 --> 00:30:03.740
want to expose them. So in the end, as it
was so many things in a democratic society,

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:07.460
quite a lot has to be taken on trust. But
that doesn't mean you can't try and verify

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:09.940
it when the opportunity arises.

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:12.809
SIERRA:
What type of position do you think we would

00:30:12.809 --> 00:30:15.690
be in as a country without this flow of intelligence?

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:19.279
HARDING:
I mean, a very difficult one. We'd be in a

00:30:19.279 --> 00:30:24.669
very difficult position. This goes back to
George Washington and the very earliest days

00:30:24.669 --> 00:30:30.010
of our country. And we talk about George Washington
as the earliest spymaster. He had a ring of

00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:34.240
spies that were called the Culper Ring, during
the revolutionary war and they were letting

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:39.289
him know what the Brits were up to, where
they were moving, he had both that tactical

00:30:39.289 --> 00:30:45.110
and that strategic intelligence. Today, I
mean, we're up against a Russian intelligence

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:51.070
service that is just as talented, if not more
so than they were in the Cold War. China has

00:30:51.070 --> 00:30:56.510
figured out, not only how to run a top-notch
intelligence service, but then also how to

00:30:56.510 --> 00:31:02.679
co-opt massive elements of society to help
those intelligence services do their work.

00:31:02.679 --> 00:31:06.919
And then, you know, there are, there are other
top-notch intel services all over the world

00:31:06.919 --> 00:31:13.710
and if we try to keep American interests strong
around the world without the insights provided

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:18.450
by the intelligence community, I think we
would have a very difficult time trying to

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:23.380
keep up with these other nations, but then
just trying to anticipate threats and preempt

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:28.940
them before they happen. We talk a lot in
the spy game about how, you know, a good day

00:31:28.940 --> 00:31:34.519
is when the bad thing does not happen because
you warned ahead of time and it was prevented

00:31:34.519 --> 00:31:38.899
by good policies. Usually, you know, what
gets out in the press is when the bad thing

00:31:38.899 --> 00:31:42.860
actually does happen and the intelligence
has failed, but we, we really enjoy the good

00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:46.291
days when something that could've been a problem
is no longer a problem because we warned about

00:31:46.291 --> 00:31:54.130
it ahead of time.

